					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2156983&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606142895" alt="psul" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2156983)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/psul" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2156983); return false;" >psul</a></span> <span class="odate time_1435770404 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Jul 2015 17:06</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2329145">
									

<p>I've given this a tentative downvote. I liked it a lot, without being completely hooked, through the end of the description - the science felt sufficiently real, it is an interesting object and it sounds rather pretty. I didn't love the 300m high cloak of invisibility, or the idea that we could detect 1cm movements through 3000km of rock, but I was prepared to roll with it.</p>
<p>But from the third paragraph of the dropdown onwards, I liked it less and less. I was uncomfortable with an ancient human deciding to use binary to communicate. I struggle to imagine a paleolithic culture that had quite such a wide vocabulary. I don't think that the crosslink to 1050 added anything (although fair play in getting permission and input). I don't know why we're communicating in gamma waves, rather than simply pushing back on the obsidian to form shapes at the far end. And I was quite disappointed at (a) the idea that this was as simple as a human intelligence in rock form; (b) the trope of &quot;hide from something worse that I can't explain but it's horrible I promise&quot;; and (c) cutting off the dialogue with &quot;lonely and stupid and horrible&quot; - it all felt quite arbitrary.</p>
<p>Sorry, that all sounds very complainy, but I feel like the ending spoiled the overall effect. I think I would upvote if this anomaly had a purpose other than a depressed human hiding from bogeymen.</p>
<p>EDIT: Okay, I should have reminded myself properly about 1050 - they're not just any bogeymen. The dialogue still doesn't quite work for me, but I'll reconsider my downvote.</p>

