<blockquote>
<p>I don't think that you are describing a real world technology here, although I could be wrong.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It isn't, at least not yet. It's the sort of thing that navies would play with (probably before other military branches), since having sufficient power and space to mess with prototype technology isn't usually a problem on ships. And I'm fairly sure most of the work they're doing (in as much as they publicly acknowledge such things) is built around countering radar rather than visual light. Cloaking devices as we think of them in fiction simply do not exist, and the closest analogues that do are sufficiently imperfect that you'll notice the hidden object if it is against a solid, not-textured background (like a clear sky). They also require obscene amounts of power, which would make an application of the sort described in the containment procedures highly impractical (and expensive).</p>
<p>Further, they would not prevent there from being a shadow from the camouflaging structure itself, which at sunrise and sunset could easily be kilometers long. Given the terrain in Katavi National Park, the Foundation has built a 200 meter tall sundial that any satellite overpass or airplane flying at standard cruising altitude for several hundred kilometers around would have no trouble seeing. (Which is another point - a 200 meter tall structure presents an aviation hazard; most places in the United States require appropriate warning lights on structures taller than 200 feet, and the International Civil Aviation Organization has global standards which most countries follow.) It's just simply not possible to make something that tall which can't be noticed by someone with an appropriate vantage point. A better solution would be to come up with some plausible structure of appropriate height (a watchtower for wildfire prevention, a radio tower, etc., though 200 meters is a bit tall for most things) and explain it away that way.</p>

