			<div class="content" id="post-content-2329495">
									

<p>Though I've stated this in PMs, for the record, I basically agree with this assessment, both in the broad sense and in the specifics. (I am also declining to vote for the time being.)</p>
<p>There are interesting things that can be done with the thinking-talking-rock idea - and it most certainly is an interesting idea, at least in theory - but I personally think that the civilization-ending-eldritch-abominations-of-doom is one of the weaker ones you could run with, especially the more explicitly you choose to do so. (I sometimes suspect that 1050 is rated as high as much in spite of that aspect as because of it.)</p>
<p>Remember the old adage about showing rather than telling. If you tell me &quot;there's a ghost in the room,&quot; I probably won't believe you and almost certainly won't be scared. If you show me what looks like a ghost in the room, I'll be more inclined to believe you, but it'd have to be pretty convincing to scare me. If you convince me that I keep seeing something out of the corner of my eye and hear the faint rustle of movement behind me, in spite of my not being able to find anything there, and suggest the last owner of the house disappeared under mysterious circumstances, then it is far more likely that you'll have set me up to scare myself. (This is <span style="text-decoration: underline;">hard</span>, which is why so many horror films use gore and jump-scares to such an extent.) So, if you can take that sort of logic and apply it to civilization-ending-things, then you're in business. The obvious flipside being that if you are too subtle, it will go right over some of your audience's heads, but that's a different problem. Unfortunately, in my opinion you haven't struck that balance.</p>
<p>Bottom line, I think the concept has lots of potential, but I recommend either pursuing a different direction from hiding-from-civilization-enders completely, or at the very least aiming to make that part more implicit than explicit (think &quot;<a href="http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/FridgeHorror">Fridge Horror</a>&quot;).</p>

							</div>

								
					
