<p>The problem, ooblex, is that if you want the reader to not take 2929-1 at its word, then you need some indication in the article to suggest that they shouldn't.</p>
<p>To put it bluntly, you're asking us to be credulous towards the fact that there is a globe-spanning, top secret organization which can make convincing-to-the-point-of-being-an-invisibility-cloak holograms that are hundreds of meters tall, and that there is a kilometers-long piece of obsidian that constantly alters its surface to create patterns clear enough to be interpreted as language (apparently generated coherently by an intelligence other than a regular old human being), and then you're asking us to be incredulous as to what this giant rock-that-writes is saying - even to the point of questioning whether or not the giant rock-that-writes is in fact anything other than pure random chance which just so happens to create symbols that correspond with an intelligible dialogue on the part of the researchers. So, would you like us to suspend disbelief, or not? The reader is likely to follow both Occam's Razor (the simplest explanation is best in the absence of evidence to the contrary) and Law of Conservation of Detail (writers rarely use up valuable space to mention irrelevant details, and the shorter the work of fiction, the less likely any particular detail is to be a product of random chance and/or not plot relevant).</p>
<p>It is true that we (both the Doylist readers and Watsonian members of the Foundation) have little corroborating evidence to suggest we ought to take 2929-1 at its word. Ten-fifty exists, and the fact that you've made a cross-link (about which I'm not complaining, mind you) gives some corroborating evidence. The fact that both give issue a warning, the shared previously known script, and that there is not an apparent shared origin (i.e., not both made by the same GoI) suggests that both should be given more serious consideration by researchers than they would be lacking that sort of corroboration. The difference is that the article for 1050 both includes details that indicate that all is not as it seems and includes clear delineations between what the Foundation knows (fact) vs thinks (educated guesses). While the article represents a prevailing viewpoint, <a href="/we-have-dismissed-that-claim">others which are mutually exclusive</a> exist which cannot be definitively debunked. All of your comments about how we should take 2929-1 with a grain of salt are in the meta-text, not the text itself. Votes reflect the article as a stand-alone piece of fiction, not (at least not necessarily) the commentary the author provides in the discussion thread.</p>

