<p>Yes. I agree. Amen. I already talked to sirpudding about appending a doctor's note to put the legitimacy of -1's claims into question. Sadly, I am leaving for vacation today and will not have access to my computer to write for more than a week. However, I can still receive and send (to a limited degree) PMs from my phone. I would be honored, Hornby, if you would write the doctors note and send it to me over my vacation so the best results are received. sirpudding could give it a shot too if he wants. If both of you decline (which you may, of course), then this will not happen for over a week. Actually, anyone could try. just send it to me for review and I will see what I can do. Thanks, and no pressure.</p>
<p>Additionally:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>apparently generated coherently by an intelligence other than a regular old human being</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, not necessarily. Maybe it <em>is</em> telling the truth. But maybe not.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>even to the point of questioning whether or not the giant rock-that-writes is in fact anything other than pure random chance which just so happens to create symbols that correspond with an intelligible dialogue on the part of the researchers.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That was a hyperbole. I didn't actually mean that, I just wanted to convey the uncertainty of the situation.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>suggests that both should be given more serious consideration by researchers than they would be lacking that sort of corroboration.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A very good point. (Disclaimer: I am not disputing this) however, do keep in mind the reasons <em>not</em> to trust 2929-1 (or even 1050). For example, is it more likely that 2929 and 2929-1 were created by <strong>very weirdly</strong> advanced Mesolithic Tanzanians, or that, say, a more plausibly<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-674575-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-674575-1')">1</a></sup> advanced entity or entities created 2929 and 2929-1 for unknown purposes? This is not a rhetorical question per se, but you (Hornby) don't need to answer it if you don't want to. It is just something to think about. In other words, if the Foundation researchers used Occam's Razor to speculate about the origins of 2929-1, would they have actually reached the conclusions they did in the final paragraph? I'm not implying an answer to that question, fyi.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the article for 1050 both includes details that indicate that all is not as it seems</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Could you please provide an example?</p>
<p>Thank you, Hornby.</p>
