<blockquote>
<p>I would be honored, Hornby, if you would write the doctors note and send it to me over my vacation so the best results are received. sirpudding could give it a shot too if he wants. If both of you decline (which you may, of course), then this will not happen for over a week. Actually, anyone could try. just send it to me for review and I will see what I can do.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This doesn't work that way. It's your article, not mine.</p>
<p>I've offered you advice and constructive criticism; I've even suggested to you a different write-up of the transcript in the log, predicated on your desire to keep the warning-of-doom aspect in this SCP (an aspect I have and continue to advocate that you either tone down or remove outright). You've taken some of that advice, and declined to take other parts, which is fine <em>because it's your article, not mine</em>.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-804605-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-804605-1')">1</a></sup> If I were (re)writing this, I'd go a different direction entirely. I don't like seeing writers fail, have a hard-learned understanding of how critical and tough-to-please this community can be, and know how discouraging it can be when blunt-to-a-fault criticism gets handed down; because of this, and because you reached out to me, I've tried to offer the best advice I can.</p>
<p>To put none too fine a point on it, I don't much like the general &quot;mind of someone hiding from civilization-ending-abominations&quot; origin/explanation as you use it in this article. It's cliche. It's cliche <em>whether or not</em> we take 2929-1 at its word (another point I'll get to in a second). Something very similar has also been done <a href="/scp-1980">before</a> elsewhere on the site<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-804605-2" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-804605-2')">2</a></sup>. I know from your PMs that you wanted to write something related to 1050, and I'm flattered that you liked that article, but I'm afraid I'm not that fond of how you've chosen to go about doing it. None of this means you can't write your article your way, but nothing says your article is going to be well-received or highly-rated (especially if you choose to ignore the advice of multiple established authors giving you the <span style="text-decoration: underline;">same</span> advice).</p>
<p>On the subject of whether or not to take -1 at its word, there needs to be something about the object itself to give the Foundation and the readers pause. Remember, SCP-2929's claims are hardly the most absurd thing the Foundation would have encountered. A simple &quot;doctor's note&quot; isn't going to cut it for most people, myself included, since it doesn't change the underlying facts surrounding the object. And <em>even if</em> we have good reason to believe that -1 isn't telling the truth, willfully or otherwise, it <span style="text-decoration: underline;">does not</span> change the fact that the stated origin is cliche.</p>
<p>So you can leave the article as is, or add a &quot;doctor's note&quot; to suggest we shouldn't automatically believe what/whoever is in the rock, or add/change details about the object itself to suggest we shouldn't automatically believe it, or you can scrap the entire Dark Ones premise in the dialogue (replacing it with something else or not). That's all up to you. I've given you my thoughts, but I'm afraid I'm not in the mood to do your creative work for you.</p>
